@ClearlyClaire yes ordering can be significant, and is part of my suspicion because one of the main changes in Ruby 3.2 is the introduction of objects shapes, which can change the ordering of instance variables.

I also see a lot of objects references in the payload, so I suspect that Status object is referenced in a Hash deeper in the payload which cause marshal to insert it before it's fully initialized.

It's just a theory at this point, but if it's correct I think I know how to produce a reproduction script

@byroot here.

Ok, so it's definitely an attribute ordering issues. I crafted a very simple repro:

```ruby
class Status
  def normal_order
    @attributes = { id: 42 }
    @relations = { self => 1 }
    self
  end

  def inverse_order
    @relations = nil
    @attributes = { id: 42 }
    @relations = { self => 1 }
    self
  end

  def hash
    @attributes.fetch(:id)
  end
end

s = Marshal.load(Marshal.dump(Status.new.normal_order))

s = Marshal.load(Marshal.dump(Status.new.inverse_order))
```

The way we can work around this is by ensuring `@attributes` is always defined first, even if that means setting it to `nil` until the actual object is set later.

I'll dig in Rails to try to figure in which condition that other order may happen, and I can get a patch merged in `6-1-stable` (can't guarantee a quick release though, but should be easy to monkey patch worst case).
